import json

STUDY_DATA = {'01_CSA': {'short': 'CSA', 'name': 'Computer System Architecture', 'code': 'ITX-201 / CSX-201', 'accent': '#bae6fd', 'tagline': 'Hardware, Datapaths, Micro-ops & Memory Hierarchy', 'units': [{'unit': 1, 'title': 'Data Representation, RTL & System Buses', 'weightage': '18%', 'topics': "Fixed/Floating Point (IEEE 754), 2's Complement, Hamming Code SECDED, Bus Arbitration (Daisy Chaining)", 'high_yield_tip': 'IEEE 754 single precision conversion (-18.625) & Hamming code (7,4) are guaranteed 5-mark questions.'}, {'unit': 2, 'title': 'CPU Arithmetic & Addressing Modes', 'weightage': '24%', 'topics': "Carry Look-Ahead vs Ripple Carry Adder, Booth's Signed Multiplication, Restoring Division, Effective Address Calculation", 'high_yield_tip': "Practice Booth's algorithm trace with step table ($Q_0, Q_{-1}$) and CLA delay calculation."}, {'unit': 3, 'title': 'Control Unit & Instruction Formats', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': 'Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control, Horizontal vs Vertical Microcode, Expanding Opcode, Instruction Cycle', 'high_yield_tip': 'Know the difference: Hardwired uses combinational logic (faster, RISC); Microprogrammed uses control memory (flexible, CISC).'}, {'unit': 4, 'title': 'Memory Organization & Cache Mapping', 'weightage': '22%', 'topics': 'Memory Hierarchy, Cache Mapping (Direct, Fully Associative, Set-Associative), LRU/FIFO Replacement, Virtual Memory & TLB', 'high_yield_tip': 'Set-Associative Tag/Set/Word bit calculation and AMAT formula $T_{avg} = h T_c + (1-h) T_m$ appear every year.'}, {'unit': 5, 'title': 'I/O Organization & Pipelining', 'weightage': '16%', 'topics': 'Programmed vs Interrupt-Driven I/O, DMA Controller (Cycle Stealing vs Burst), Pipelining Hazards & Speedup', 'high_yield_tip': '3 Pipeline hazards (Structural, RAW/WAR/WAW Data, Control) and Branch Prediction formulas.'}], 'formulas': [{'title': 'IEEE 754 Single-Precision Floating Point', 'latex': 'V = (-1)^S \\times (1.M) \\times 2^{E - 127}', 'unit': 'Unit 1', 'explanation': 'Sign bit (1 bit), Biased Exponent (8 bits with Bias = 127), Fraction/Mantissa (23 bits with implicit leading 1).', 'trap': "Remember to add 127 to the real power of 2 when encoding E! Don't write the 1 before the binary point into the 23-bit mantissa.", 'example': 'For $-18.625$: $S=1$, $18.625_{10} = 10010.101_2 = 1.0010101 \\times 2^4$. $E = 4 + 127 = 131 = 10000011_2$. Hex = 0xC1950000.'}, {'title': "Amdahl's Law Speedup", 'latex': 'S_{overall} = \\frac{1}{(1 - f) + \\frac{f}{k}}', 'unit': 'Unit 1', 'explanation': '$f$ is the fraction of execution time enhanced, and $k$ is the speedup factor of that portion.', 'trap': 'Even if $k \\to \\infty$, the maximum theoretical speedup is bounded by $\\frac{1}{1-f}$.', 'example': 'If 80% ($f=0.8$) of a program is made 4x faster ($k=4$): $S = 1 / (0.2 + 0.8/4) = 1 / 0.4 = 2.5\\times$.'}, {'title': 'Pipelined Processor Speedup', 'latex': 'S_k = \\frac{n \\cdot k}{k + n - 1} \\xrightarrow{n \\gg k} k', 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'explanation': 'Speedup for $n$ instructions executed in a $k$-stage pipeline compared to non-pipelined execution.', 'trap': 'Stalls and hazards reduce actual speedup: $CPI_{pipe} = 1 + \\text{Stalls per Instruction}$.', 'example': 'For 1000 instructions on a 5-stage pipeline: $S = (1000 \\times 5) / (5 + 999) = 5000 / 1004 \\approx 4.98\\times$.'}, {'title': 'Average Memory Access Time (AMAT)', 'latex': 'T_{avg} = T_{L1} + (1 - h_1) \\cdot \\text{Penalty}_{L1}', 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'explanation': 'Hierarchical memory latency where $h_1$ is L1 hit rate and $(1-h_1)$ is miss rate.', 'trap': 'In 2-level cache: $\\text{Penalty}_{L1} = T_{L2} + (1 - h_2) \\cdot T_{MainMemory}$.', 'example': 'If $T_{L1} = 1\\text{ns}$, $h_1 = 0.95$, miss penalty = $50\\text{ns}$: $T_{avg} = 1 + 0.05 \\times 50 = 3.5\\text{ns}$.'}, {'title': "Booth's Algorithm Action Rule", 'latex': 'Q_0 Q_{-1} \\implies \\begin{cases} 10: & A \\leftarrow A - M \\\\ 01: & A \\leftarrow A + M \\\\ 00, 11: & \\text{No op (Arithmetic Shift Right)} \\end{cases}', 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'explanation': "Multiplication of signed 2's complement numbers. Arithmetic shift right (ASR) preserves the sign bit of A.", 'trap': 'Always initialize $Q_{-1} = 0$ and $A = 0000$. Count cycles equal to number of bits in multiplier.', 'example': 'For $-5 \\times 3$: $M = 1011$ (-5), $Q = 0011$ (3), $Q_{-1} = 0$. In step 1: $Q_0 Q_{-1} = 10 \\Rightarrow A = A - M$, then ASR.'}, {'title': 'Set-Associative Cache Field Breakdown', 'latex': '\\text{Address} = \\underbrace{\\text{Tag}}_{(N - S - W)} + \\underbrace{\\text{Index/Set}}_{S = \\log_2(\\text{Sets})} + \\underbrace{\\text{Offset}}_{W = \\log_2(\\text{Block Size})}', 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'explanation': 'Formula for dividing physical memory address into Tag, Set index, and Word/Byte offset.', 'trap': 'Number of sets = $\\text{Total Cache Size} / (k \\times \\text{Block Size})$ for a $k$-way set-associative cache.', 'example': '32-bit address, 64 KB cache, 4-way associative, 64-byte blocks. Sets = $64\\text{KB} / (4 \\times 64\\text{B}) = 256$ ($S=8$ bits). Offset $W=6$ bits. Tag = $32 - 8 - 6 = 18$ bits.'}], 'flashcards': [{'q': 'What is the difference between Spatial Locality and Temporal Locality of reference?', 'a': '• **Spatial Locality**: If a memory location is accessed, nearby memory locations are likely to be accessed soon (exploited by fetching entire cache blocks/lines).\n• **Temporal Locality**: If a memory location is accessed once, it is likely to be accessed again in the near future (e.g. loops, counters; exploited by keeping recently accessed blocks in fast cache via LRU).', 'marks': '3–4 Marks', 'frequency': 'High (Asked in 2024, 2025)'}, {'q': 'Differentiate between Hardwired Control Unit and Microprogrammed Control Unit.', 'a': '1. **Architecture**: Hardwired uses pure combinational logic gates/flip-flops; Microprogrammed uses a Control Memory (ROM) storing micro-routines.\n2. **Speed**: Hardwired is significantly faster (direct gate propagation delay); Microprogrammed is slower due to ROM fetch cycle.\n3. **Flexibility**: Hardwired is difficult to modify/expand (requires rewiring); Microprogrammed is easy to modify by updating control microcode.\n4. **Application**: Hardwired in RISC processors (ARM, MIPS); Microprogrammed in complex CISC processors (x86).', 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Very High'}, {'q': 'Explain the 3 main types of Pipeline Hazards and how each is resolved.', 'a': '1. **Structural Hazard**: Hardware resource conflict (e.g. single memory for both instruction and data). Resolved by: Separate I-cache and D-cache (Harvard architecture) or inserting pipeline bubbles.\n2. **Data Hazard (RAW, WAR, WAW)**: Instruction depends on result of a previous instruction still in pipeline. Resolved by: Operand Forwarding / Bypassing, compiler instruction reordering, or hardware stalls.\n3. **Control Hazard (Branch Hazard)**: Branch condition and target address not known until later stage. Resolved by: Delayed branching, Branch Target Buffer (BTB), static/dynamic Branch Prediction.', 'marks': '6 Marks', 'frequency': 'Very High'}, {'q': 'Why is a Carry Look-Ahead (CLA) Adder faster than a Ripple Carry Adder (RCA)?', 'a': 'In an $n$-bit Ripple Carry Adder, each full adder must wait for the carry bit ($C_i$) from the preceding stage, resulting in an $O(n)$ propagation delay ($2n$ gate levels).\nIn a Carry Look-Ahead Adder, all carries are generated in parallel in $O(1)$ time (constant 2 gate levels) using Generate ($G_i = A_i B_i$) and Propagate ($P_i = A_i \\oplus B_i$) functions: $C_{i+1} = G_i + P_i C_i$.', 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Guaranteed'}, {'q': 'Explain Direct Memory Access (DMA) and differentiate Burst Mode from Cycle Stealing.', 'a': 'DMA allows high-speed I/O devices (disk, network) to transfer data directly to/from main memory without routing through the CPU, freeing the CPU for computation.\n• **Burst Mode (Block Transfer)**: DMA controller gains bus mastership and transfers an entire block of data continuously while holding the bus.\n• **Cycle Stealing**: DMA controller takes control of the bus for only one memory cycle at a time during CPU internal execution states, minimizing CPU interruption.', 'marks': '4–5 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}], 'exam_questions': [{'id': 'csa_q1', 'marks': 2, 'unit': 'Unit 1', 'title': "Overflow Detection in 2's Complement", 'question': "State the exact condition for arithmetic overflow when adding two signed n-bit 2's complement numbers. Write the Boolean formula in terms of carries.", 'hint': 'Consider the sign bits of the operands and the carry-in / carry-out of the sign bit position.', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Explanation that overflow occurs when adding two numbers of identical signs produces an opposite sign result', '1 Mark: Boolean formula V = C_in XOR C_out of the sign bit'], 'model_answer': '**Condition:** Overflow occurs if and only if two numbers of the same sign are added and produce a result with the opposite sign ($(+) + (+) = (-)$ or $(-) + (-) = (+)$).\n\n**Boolean Formula:**\n$$V = C_n \\oplus C_{n-1}$$\nwhere $C_n$ is carry-out of MSB (sign bit) and $C_{n-1}$ is carry-in into MSB. If $V = 1$, overflow is detected.', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Sept 2026 Mid-Sem Q2(a)'}, {'id': 'csa_q2', 'marks': 5, 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'title': "Booth's Signed Multiplication Trace", 'question': "Multiply (-5) by (+3) using Booth's Algorithm in 4-bit 2's complement arithmetic. Show the step-by-step register trace table with A, Q, Q_{-1}, and operation.", 'hint': 'M = 1011 (-5), -M = 0101 (+5), Q = 0011 (+3), Q_{-1} = 0. In each step inspect Q_0 Q_{-1}.', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Proper binary initialization (M, -M, A=0000, Q=0011, Q_-1=0)', '2 Marks: Correct operation identification for 10 (A = A - M) and 01 (A = A + M)', '1 Mark: Correct Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR) maintaining sign bit of A', '1 Mark: Final correct 8-bit product (11110001_2 = -15_10)'], 'model_answer': '**Setup:** $M = 1011$ (-5), $-M = 0101$ (+5), $Q = 0011$ (+3), $A = 0000$, $Q_{-1} = 0$, Count = 4.\n\n| Step | Operation | A | Q | $Q_{-1}$ | Count |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| 0 | Initial | `0000` | `0011` | `0` | 4 |\n| 1 | $Q_0 Q_{-1}=10 \\to A = A - M$ | `0101` | `0011` | `0` | 4 |\n| 1 | ASR | `0010` | `1001` | `1` | 3 |\n| 2 | $Q_0 Q_{-1}=11 \\to$ No op | `0010` | `1001` | `1` | 3 |\n| 2 | ASR | `0001` | `0100` | `1` | 2 |\n| 3 | $Q_0 Q_{-1}=01 \\to A = A + M$ | `1100` | `0100` | `1` | 2 |\n| 3 | ASR | `1110` | `0010` | `0` | 1 |\n| 4 | $Q_0 Q_{-1}=00 \\to$ No op | `1110` | `0010` | `0` | 1 |\n| 4 | ASR | `1111` | `0001` | `0` | 0 |\n\n**Result:** $AQ = 11110001_2 = -15_{10}$. Verified correct! ($-5 \\times 3 = -15$).', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Sept 2025 Mid-Sem Q4'}, {'id': 'csa_q3', 'marks': 10, 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'title': 'Set-Associative Cache Organization & Field Math', 'question': "A system has a 32-bit physical address, a 4-way set-associative cache with 64 KB total cache data capacity, and 32-byte block size.\n(a) Calculate the number of bits in Tag, Set/Index, and Word Offset.\n(b) If Average Memory Access Time (AMAT) is 2.8 ns with L1 hit time 1 ns and miss penalty 60 ns, find the cache hit ratio $h$.\n(c) Compare FIFO vs LRU replacement policies in terms of hardware overhead and Belady's anomaly.", 'hint': 'Total Blocks = Cache Size / Block Size. Total Sets = Total Blocks / k-ways.', 'marking_scheme': ['3 Marks: Correct bit calculation: Offset = 5 bits, Set = 9 bits, Tag = 18 bits', '3 Marks: AMAT equation: 2.8 = 1 + (1-h)*60 => h = 97%', "4 Marks: FIFO vs LRU: FIFO uses counters, suffers from Belady's anomaly; LRU tracks recency, Belady-free stack algorithm"], 'model_answer': "**Part (a): Bit Breakdown**\n- Offset $W$: $\\log_2(32) = 5$ bits.\n- Total Blocks = $64\\text{ KB} / 32\\text{ B} = 2^{16} / 2^5 = 2048$ blocks.\n- Sets $S$ (4-way): $2048 / 4 = 512 = 2^9$ sets $\\implies 9$ bits for Set Index.\n- Tag = $32 - 9 - 5 = 18$ bits.\n- **Address format:** Tag (18 bits) | Set (9 bits) | Offset (5 bits).\n\n**Part (b): Hit Ratio Calculation**\n$$T_{avg} = T_{hit} + (1 - h) \\times M_{penalty}$$\n$$2.8 = 1.0 + (1 - h) \\times 60 \\implies 1.8 = 60(1 - h) \\implies 1 - h = 0.03$$\n$$h = 0.97 = 97\\%$$\n\n**Part (c): FIFO vs LRU**\n- **LRU**: Replaces the block unreferenced for the longest time. Stack algorithm; guaranteed free from Belady's Anomaly. Requires $\\log_2(k!)$ bits or age matrix.\n- **FIFO**: Replaces oldest block regardless of recent access. Can suffer from Belady's Anomaly where increasing cache size increases miss rate.", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2025 End-Sem Q4(a),(b)'}]}, '02_DBMS': {'short': 'DBMS', 'name': 'Database Management Systems', 'code': 'CSX-205 / ITX-205', 'accent': '#a7f3d0', 'tagline': 'Relational Models, SQL, Normalization, ACID & Indexing', 'units': [{'unit': 1, 'title': 'Database Architecture & ER Modeling', 'weightage': '15%', 'topics': '3-Schema Architecture, Data Independence, ER & EER Diagrams, Key Constraints (Candidate, Super, Primary, Foreign)', 'high_yield_tip': 'Be ready to convert an ER diagram with M:N relationship, weak entity, and multi-valued attributes into relational tables.'}, {'unit': 2, 'title': 'Relational Algebra & Advanced SQL', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': 'Selection, Projection, Cartesian Product, Natural Join, Outer Joins, Division, Nested Subqueries, Aggregations, Views', 'high_yield_tip': "Relational algebra division operator ($R \\div S$) tests 'for all' conditions in every end-term exam."}, {'unit': 3, 'title': 'Functional Dependencies & Normalization', 'weightage': '25%', 'topics': 'Closure of Attribute Set, Minimal Cover, 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF, Lossless Join Test, Dependency Preservation', 'high_yield_tip': 'Finding candidate keys from attribute closure and testing BCNF/3NF violation is a 10-mark compulsory question.'}, {'unit': 4, 'title': 'Transaction Processing & Concurrency', 'weightage': '22%', 'topics': 'ACID Properties, Schedules, Conflict Serializability (Precedence Graph), View Serializability, 2PL (Strict & Rigorous 2PL)', 'high_yield_tip': 'Drawing the precedence graph to prove conflict serializability and finding equivalent serial schedule.'}, {'unit': 5, 'title': 'Storage, B/B+ Trees & Recovery', 'weightage': '18%', 'topics': 'File Organizations, Dense vs Sparse Indexes, B-Tree & B+ Tree insertions/deletions, ARIES Recovery & WAL protocol', 'high_yield_tip': 'Why B+ Trees are superior to B Trees for databases (range queries, leaf node linked list, compact internal keys).'}], 'formulas': [{'title': 'Normal Forms Classification Rules', 'latex': '\\text{BCNF} \\subset \\text{3NF} \\subset \\text{2NF} \\subset \\text{1NF}', 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'explanation': '• 1NF: All attributes atomic.\n• 2NF: 1NF + No partial dependency (proper subset of candidate key determining non-prime).\n• 3NF: 2NF + For every $X \\to Y$, $X$ is a superkey OR $Y$ is a prime attribute.\n• BCNF: For every $X \\to Y$, $X$ MUST be a superkey.', 'trap': '3NF always guarantees both lossless join and dependency preservation, but BCNF may not preserve functional dependencies!', 'example': 'Relation $R(A, B, C)$ with $AB \\to C$ and $C \\to B$. Candidate keys: $AB, AC$. $C \\to B$ violates BCNF ($C$ not superkey), but is in 3NF ($B$ is prime).'}, {'title': 'Conflict Serializability Precedence Graph Rule', 'latex': '\\text{Schedule } S \\text{ is Conflict Serializable} \\iff \\mathcal{G}(S) \\text{ is an Acyclic Directed Graph}', 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'explanation': 'Create node for each transaction $T_i$. Draw directed edge $T_i \\to T_j$ if $T_i$ executes a conflicting operation on data item $Q$ before $T_j$.', 'trap': 'Conflicting operations: at least one must be a WRITE ($W_i(Q)$ before $R_j(Q)$, $R_i(Q)$ before $W_j(Q)$, or $W_i(Q)$ before $W_j(Q)$). Two READs never conflict!', 'example': 'If $T_1: W(A)$, $T_2: R(A) \\Rightarrow T_1 \\to T_2$. If later $T_2: W(B)$, $T_1: R(B) \\Rightarrow T_2 \\to T_1$. Cycle exists $\\Rightarrow$ NOT conflict serializable.'}, {'title': 'Lossless Join Decomposition Condition', 'latex': '(R_1 \\cap R_2) \\to (R_1 - R_2) \\quad \\text{OR} \\quad (R_1 \\cap R_2) \\to (R_2 - R_1)', 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'explanation': 'Decomposition of $R$ into $R_1$ and $R_2$ is lossless if and only if the common attribute contains a candidate key for at least one of the relations.', 'trap': 'If the intersection attribute closure does NOT cover $R_1$ or $R_2$, it is a lossy decomposition (spurious tuples produced on join).', 'example': '$R(A,B,C)$ decomposed into $R_1(A,B)$ and $R_2(B,C)$. Common attribute is $B$. If $B \\to C$ holds in $F^+$, decomposition is lossless!'}, {'title': 'B+ Tree Node Properties (Order m)', 'latex': '\\text{Root: } [1, m-1] \\text{ keys}; \\quad \\text{Internal: } \\left[ \\lceil m/2 \\rceil - 1, m-1 \\right] \\text{ keys}', 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'explanation': 'Max pointers = $m$, Max keys = $m-1$. Min pointers in internal node = $\\lceil m/2 \\rceil$, Min keys = $\\lceil m/2 \\rceil - 1$.', 'trap': 'Leaf nodes store actual record pointers/data; internal nodes only store routing keys and child node pointers.', 'example': 'For order $m=5$: Max keys = 4, Min keys in non-root internal node = $\\lceil 5/2 \\rceil - 1 = 3 - 1 = 2$ keys.'}], 'flashcards': [{'q': 'Why are B+ Trees preferred over B-Trees for database index structures?', 'a': "1. **Faster Range Queries**: All leaf nodes in a B+ Tree are linked together in a doubly linked list, allowing efficient sequential scan without tree re-traversals.\n2. **Higher Fanout & Shallower Tree**: Internal nodes only store keys (no record pointers), fitting far more keys per disk block and reducing tree height (fewer disk I/Os).\n3. **Uniform Search Time**: Every key search must traverse down to a leaf node, guaranteeing consistent $O(\\log n)$ search latency.\n4. **Simpler Deletion**: Deleting from a leaf node doesn't require complex internal rebalancing as long as the minimum key condition holds.", 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Extremely High (Asked almost every year)'}, {'q': 'What is the Two-Phase Locking (2PL) protocol? Differentiate Basic, Strict, and Rigorous 2PL.', 'a': '2PL guarantees conflict serializability by requiring transactions to acquire locks in a **Growing Phase** and release them in a **Shrinking Phase** (no locks acquired after any lock released).\n• **Basic 2PL**: Locks released anytime during shrinking phase. Prone to cascading aborts!\n• **Strict 2PL**: All exclusive (X) locks held until transaction commits/aborts. Prevents cascading rollbacks!\n• **Rigorous 2PL**: BOTH shared (S) and exclusive (X) locks held until commit/abort. Guarantees serializability and simplifies recovery.', 'marks': '6 Marks', 'frequency': 'Very High'}, {'q': 'Explain the ACID properties of database transactions with real-world examples.', 'a': "• **A - Atomicity**: 'All or nothing'. Either all operations of transaction complete or none do (managed by Recovery Manager & Undo Log).\n• **C - Consistency**: Database must transition from one valid state to another, satisfying all integrity constraints (managed by DBMS and application logic).\n• **I - Isolation**: Concurrently executing transactions must not interfere with one another (managed by Concurrency Control / Locking).\n• **D - Durability**: Once committed, changes survive system crashes or power failures (managed by Write-Ahead Logging WAL / Redo Log).", 'marks': '4 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}, {'q': 'What is the Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) protocol and why is it essential?', 'a': 'WAL requires that:\n1. The log record for an update must be written to stable non-volatile storage **BEFORE** the corresponding database page is flushed to disk.\n2. A transaction cannot commit until all its log records (including the commit record) have been written to disk.\nIt is essential because if a crash occurs after updating a page in RAM, the DBMS can reconstruct committed changes (Redo) and rollback uncommitted transactions (Undo).', 'marks': '4 Marks', 'frequency': 'Medium-High'}], 'exam_questions': [{'id': 'dbms_q1', 'marks': 2, 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'title': 'Super Key vs Candidate Key', 'question': 'Differentiate between a Super Key and a Candidate Key in Relational Database Theory. Can every Super Key be a Candidate Key?', 'hint': 'Recall the property of minimality (irreducibility).', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Super key is any set of attributes with unique identification; Candidate key is a minimal super key', '1 Mark: Clear negative answer explaining that not all super keys are minimal'], 'model_answer': '• **Super Key**: Any set of one or more attributes that uniquely identifies a tuple within a relation. It may contain redundant attributes.\n• **Candidate Key**: A **minimal** super key—a super key from which no attribute can be removed without losing the uniqueness property.\n• **Conclusion**: Every candidate key is a super key, but **not every super key is a candidate key** (e.g. `{RollNo, Name}` is a super key, but not a candidate key if `{RollNo}` suffices).', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar End-Term Conceptual Core'}, {'id': 'dbms_q2', 'marks': 5, 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'title': 'BCNF vs 3NF Decomposition & Dependency Preservation', 'question': 'Given relation $R(A, B, C, D)$ with FDs: $F = \\{A \\to B, \\; B \\to C, \\; C \\to D, \\; D \\to A\\}$. Determine the candidate keys. Is $R$ in BCNF? Explain whether 3NF always guarantees dependency preservation while BCNF may not.', 'hint': 'Compute attribute closures for each attribute.', 'marking_scheme': ['2 Marks: Candidate keys calculation (A, B, C, D are all candidate keys)', '1 Mark: BCNF check (every determinant is a candidate/super key => in BCNF)', '2 Marks: Clear theorem statement: 3NF lossless + dependency preserving is always achievable; BCNF guarantees lossless but may sacrifice dependencies'], 'model_answer': '**1. Candidate Key Computation:**\n- $A^+ = \\{A, B, C, D\\} \\implies A$ is a candidate key.\n- $B^+ = \\{B, C, D, A\\} \\implies B$ is a candidate key.\n- $C^+ = \\{C, D, A, B\\} \\implies C$ is a candidate key.\n- $D^+ = \\{D, A, B, C\\} \\implies D$ is a candidate key.\n\n**2. BCNF Check:**\nIn all FDs ($A \\to B, B \\to C, C \\to D, D \\to A$), the left-hand side is a candidate key. Therefore, **$R$ is strictly in BCNF**.\n\n**3. 3NF vs BCNF Guarantee:**\n- **3NF** always guarantees BOTH lossless-join decomposition AND functional dependency preservation.\n- **BCNF** guarantees lossless join, but can **lose functional dependencies** (classic example: $R(J, K, L)$ with $JK \\to L$ and $L \\to K$; BCNF decomposition loses $JK \\to L$).', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2024 End-Sem Q3'}, {'id': 'dbms_q3', 'marks': 10, 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'title': 'Concurrency Control & Precedence Graph Serializability', 'question': 'Consider schedule $S$ with transactions $T_1, T_2, T_3$:\n$$S: r_1(X); \\; r_2(Z); \\; r_1(Z); \\; r_3(X); \\; w_1(X); \\; w_3(Y); \\; w_2(Z); \\; w_2(Y)$$\n(a) Construct the Precedence (Serialization) Graph for $S$.\n(b) Determine whether $S$ is Conflict Serializable. If yes, state an equivalent serial order.\n(c) Explain the Strict Two-Phase Locking (Strict 2PL) protocol and prove why it avoids cascading aborts.', 'hint': 'Check all conflicting operation pairs: r_i(A)/w_j(A), w_i(A)/r_j(A), w_i(A)/w_j(A) for i != j.', 'marking_scheme': ['4 Marks: Correct identification of conflicting pairs & edges in precedence graph', '2 Marks: Cycle detection analysis & serial order declaration', '4 Marks: Strict 2PL explanation: holds all exclusive locks until commit/abort => avoids cascading aborts'], 'model_answer': '**Part (a): Conflicting Operations Analysis**\n1. On $X$:\n   - $r_3(X)$ occurs before $w_1(X) \\implies$ Edge $T_3 \\to T_1$.\n2. On $Z$:\n   - $r_1(Z)$ occurs before $w_2(Z) \\implies$ Edge $T_1 \\to T_2$.\n3. On $Y$:\n   - $w_3(Y)$ occurs before $w_2(Y) \\implies$ Edge $T_3 \\to T_2$.\n\nEdges in Precedence Graph: $\\{T_3 \\to T_1, \\; T_1 \\to T_2, \\; T_3 \\to T_2\\}$.\n\n**Part (b): Conflict Serializability**\nThe graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) with **NO cycles**!\nTherefore, schedule $S$ is **Conflict Serializable**.\n**Equivalent Serial Order:** $T_3 \\longrightarrow T_1 \\longrightarrow T_2$.\n\n**Part (c): Strict 2PL & Cascading Aborts**\n- In **Strict 2PL**, all exclusive (write) locks acquired by a transaction must be held until the transaction finishes (commit or abort).\n- **Why it avoids Cascading Aborts:** No transaction $T_j$ can read dirty uncommitted data written by $T_i$ because $T_i$ holds its exclusive lock on the data until after it commits. Thus, an abort of $T_i$ never forces any other transaction to roll back.', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2025 End-Sem Q2'}]}, '03_DSA': {'short': 'DSA', 'name': 'Data Structures & Algorithms', 'code': 'CSX-201 / ITX-201', 'accent': '#fed7aa', 'tagline': 'Asymptotic Analysis, Trees, Graphs & Dynamic Programming', 'units': [{'unit': 1, 'title': 'Asymptotic Analysis & Linear Structures', 'weightage': '18%', 'topics': 'Big-O, Omega, Theta notations, Master Theorem (all cases), Linked Lists, Circular & Doubly Linked Lists', 'high_yield_tip': 'Master Theorem variations with $\\log^k n$ extensions always appear in mid-sem and end-sem exams.'}, {'unit': 2, 'title': 'Stacks, Queues & Priority Queues', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': "Infix to Postfix conversion using stack, Monotonic Stacks, Binary Min/Max Heaps, Heap Sort & Floyd's $O(n)$ Build Heap", 'high_yield_tip': 'Mathematical proof that building a heap takes $O(n)$ time using $\\sum \\frac{h}{2^h} = 2$.'}, {'unit': 3, 'title': 'Trees & Self-Balancing BSTs', 'weightage': '24%', 'topics': 'Binary Tree properties ($n_0 = n_2 + 1$ proof), AVL Tree Rotations (LL, RR, LR, RL), Red-Black Tree 5 Properties & Invariants', 'high_yield_tip': 'Trace AVL insertions with balance factors: LL/RR single rotation, LR/RL double rotation.'}, {'unit': 4, 'title': 'Graph Algorithms & Shortest Paths', 'weightage': '22%', 'topics': "BFS/DFS, Topological Sort (Kahn's), Dijkstra's, Bellman-Ford (negative cycles), Prim's and Kruskal's MST (Disjoint Set Union)", 'high_yield_tip': 'Dijkstra fails with negative edges; Bellman-Ford detects negative weight cycles in $O(VE)$.'}, {'unit': 5, 'title': 'Algorithm Design & Dynamic Programming', 'weightage': '16%', 'topics': 'Greedy (Fractional Knapsack, Huffman), DP: 0/1 Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence (LCS), Matrix Chain Multiplication', 'high_yield_tip': 'Writing the exact recurrence relation and filling the bottom-up DP table for 0/1 Knapsack & LCS.'}], 'formulas': [{'title': 'Master Theorem for Divide & Conquer', 'latex': 'T(n) = aT(n/b) + \\Theta(n^k \\log^p n) \\quad (a \\ge 1, b > 1)', 'unit': 'Unit 1', 'explanation': '• Case 1: If $k < \\log_b a \\implies T(n) = \\Theta(n^{\\log_b a})$\n• Case 2: If $k = \\log_b a$:\n  - If $p > -1 \\implies T(n) = \\Theta(n^k \\log^{p+1} n)$\n  - If $p = -1 \\implies T(n) = \\Theta(n^k \\log \\log n)$\n• Case 3: If $k > \\log_b a$:\n  - If $p \\ge 0 \\implies T(n) = \\Theta(n^k \\log^p n)$', 'trap': 'Master theorem cannot be applied if $a$ is not constant, $b < 1$, or $f(n)$ is not a polynomial.', 'example': 'For Merge Sort: $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) \\implies a=2, b=2, k=1$. Since $\\log_2 2 = 1 = k \\implies$ Case 2 $\\implies O(n \\log n)$.'}, {'title': 'Binary Tree Leaf Nodes Proof', 'latex': 'n_0 = n_2 + 1', 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'explanation': 'In any non-empty binary tree, the number of leaf nodes ($n_0$) is always strictly one more than the number of nodes with two children ($n_2$).', 'trap': 'Nodes with degree 1 ($n_1$) do not affect the difference between $n_0$ and $n_2$!', 'example': 'Proof: Total nodes $n = n_0 + n_1 + n_2$. Total edges $e = n - 1$. Also $e = 0 \\cdot n_0 + 1 \\cdot n_1 + 2 \\cdot n_2$. Equating gives $n_0 + n_1 + n_2 - 1 = n_1 + 2n_2 \\implies n_0 = n_2 + 1$.'}, {'title': 'AVL Tree Balance Factor & Height Bound', 'latex': 'BF(v) = \\text{height}(v.left) - \\text{height}(v.right) \\in \\{-1, 0, +1\\}; \\quad h < 1.44 \\log_2(n + 2)', 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'explanation': 'Every node must have balance factor $-1, 0,$ or $+1$. If $|BF| \\ge 2$, perform LL/RR (single) or LR/RL (double) rotation.', 'trap': 'LR rotation = Left rotation on left child, followed by Right rotation on unbalanced node.', 'example': 'If left child has $BF = +1$ (LL case), single right rotation restores balance in $O(1)$ time.'}, {'title': '0/1 Knapsack DP Recurrence', 'latex': 'DP[i][w] = \\begin{cases} DP[i-1][w] & \\text{if } wt[i] > w \\\\ \\max(DP[i-1][w], \\; val[i] + DP[i-1][w - wt[i]]) & \\text{if } wt[i] \\le w \\end{cases}', 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'explanation': '$DP[i][w]$ represents maximum profit considering first $i$ items with capacity $w$. Time complexity is $O(n W)$ (pseudo-polynomial).', 'trap': 'Greedy by value/weight ratio fails for 0/1 Knapsack (only works for Fractional Knapsack).', 'example': 'Item wt=3, val=4, capacity $w=5$. Choice is $\\max(DP[i-1][5], 4 + DP[i-1][2])$.'}], 'flashcards': [{'q': "Why does building a binary heap take $O(n)$ time using Floyd's algorithm instead of $O(n \\log n)$?", 'a': "Inserting $n$ elements one by one takes $\\sum \\log i = O(n \\log n)$.\nFloyd's bottom-up `build_heap` runs `heapify` from $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ down to 1. Most nodes are near the bottom of the tree where height $h$ is small:\n$$\\text{Total Work} = \\sum_{h=0}^{\\lfloor \\log n \\rfloor} \\left( \\frac{n}{2^{h+1}} \\right) O(h) = O(n) \\sum_{h=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{h}{2^h} = O(n) \\times 2 = O(n).$$", 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Extremely High (Standard NITJ theoretical question)'}, {'q': "When does Dijkstra's algorithm fail and why can Bellman-Ford handle negative weight edges?", 'a': "• **Dijkstra's Failure**: Dijkstra assumes that once a vertex is extracted from the priority queue, its shortest distance is finalized (greedy choice). If a negative edge exists later in the graph, a shorter path to an already finalized node could exist, which Dijkstra will never re-evaluate.\n• **Bellman-Ford**: Relaxes all $|E|$ edges $|V|-1$ times, progressively propagating shortest path lengths up to $|V|-1$ edges. A $V$-th pass can detect negative-weight cycles if any distance further decreases.", 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}, {'q': 'State the 5 essential Red-Black Tree invariants.', 'a': '1. Every node is either **RED** or **BLACK**.\n2. The **root** is always **BLACK**.\n3. All **leaves (NIL)** are **BLACK**.\n4. If a node is **RED**, both its children must be **BLACK** (No two consecutive red nodes along any path).\n5. For each node, every simple path from this node to any descendant NIL leaf contains the **same number of black nodes** (Black-Height).', 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}], 'exam_questions': [{'id': 'dsa_q1', 'marks': 2, 'unit': 'Unit 1', 'title': 'QuickSort Best vs Worst Case Asymptotic Bounds', 'question': 'What is the time complexity of QuickSort in the Best, Average, and Worst case? What pivot choice triggers the worst-case behavior in standard Hoare/Lomuto partitioning?', 'hint': 'Think about already sorted or reverse sorted arrays.', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Best & Average O(n log n), Worst O(n^2)', '1 Mark: Worst-case condition: choosing extreme elements (first or last) as pivot on already sorted/reverse-sorted inputs'], 'model_answer': '• **Best Case:** $\\Omega(n \\log n)$ — when pivot divides array into two equal halves ($T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n)$).\n• **Average Case:** $\\Theta(n \\log n)$ — random permutations.\n• **Worst Case:** $O(n^2)$ — when pivot is repeatedly the minimum or maximum element, creating a degenerate partition of size $0$ and $n-1$ ($T(n) = T(n-1) + O(n)$).\n• **Trigger:** Selecting the first or last element as pivot on an already sorted or reverse sorted array.', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar End-Term Algorithm Core'}, {'id': 'dsa_q2', 'marks': 5, 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'title': 'AVL Tree Balancing & Rotations Trace', 'question': 'Insert keys `[10, 20, 30, 25, 28]` into an initially empty AVL Tree. Show the tree after each imbalance, identify the required rotation (LL, RR, LR, RL), and draw the final balanced AVL tree.', 'hint': 'Check balance factors: BF = height(left) - height(right). Allowed BF in AVL is {-1, 0, +1}.', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Insert 10, 20, 30; detect RR imbalance at 10; execute Left Rotation (RR)', '2 Marks: Insert 25; detect RL imbalance at 30; execute Right-Left double rotation', '2 Marks: Insert 28; final balanced tree verified with all balance factors in {-1, 0, +1}'], 'model_answer': "**Step 1: Insert 10, 20, 30**\n- `10 -> 20 -> 30` creates RR imbalance at node 10 ($BF = -2$).\n- Apply **Left Rotation (RR)** at node 10:\n  - Root becomes `20`, left child `10`, right child `30`.\n\n**Step 2: Insert 25**\n- `25` goes to left of `30`. Tree: `20` root, left `10`, right `30`, right->left `25`.\n- $BF(20) = 1 - 2 = -1$; $BF(30) = 1 - 0 = +1$. Balanced!\n\n**Step 3: Insert 28**\n- `28` goes to right of `25`. Subtree at 30: `30` -> left `25` -> right `28`.\n- Node 30 has $BF = +2$, left child 25 has $BF = -1$. **RL Imbalance!**\n- **Right-Left Double Rotation at 30:**\n  1. Left-rotate at 25: `28` becomes parent of `25`.\n  2. Right-rotate at 30: `28` becomes root of subtree, left child `25`, right child `30`.\n\n**Final AVL Tree:**\n- Root: `20`\n- Left Child: `10`\n- Right Child: `28`\n- `28`'s Left: `25`; `28`'s Right: `30`\nAll nodes have balance factors $0$ or $-1$. Perfectly balanced!", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2025 End-Sem Q3(a)'}, {'id': 'dsa_q3', 'marks': 10, 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'title': '0/1 Knapsack: Dynamic Programming vs Greedy Strategy', 'question': '(a) State the 0/1 Knapsack Problem and write its optimal substructure recurrence relation.\n(b) Solve for capacity $W = 7$ with items: Weights $w = [2, 3, 4, 5]$ and Values $v = [3, 4, 5, 8]$. Construct the complete DP table $K[i, w]$.\n(c) Backtrack to find the exact subset of items included in the optimal knapsack.\n(d) Explain why the Greedy strategy (by value/weight ratio) works for Fractional Knapsack but fails for 0/1 Knapsack.', 'hint': 'DP Recurrence: K[i, w] = max(K[i-1, w], v_i + K[i-1, w - w_i]) if w >= w_i else K[i-1, w].', 'marking_scheme': ['2 Marks: Mathematical DP recurrence definition with base cases', '4 Marks: Correct 4x8 DP matrix computation', '2 Marks: Backtracking path identifying items {Item 2 (wt 3, val 4) and Item 4 (wt 5, val 8) -> total wt 8? No, Item 1 and Item 4 => wt 7, val 11}', '2 Marks: Rigorous counterexample showing greedy choice failure in 0/1'], 'model_answer': '**Part (a): Recurrence Relation**\n$$K[i, w] = \\begin{cases} 0 & \\text{if } i=0 \\text{ or } w=0 \\\\ K[i-1, w] & \\text{if } w_i > w \\\\ \\max(K[i-1, w], \\; v_i + K[i-1, w - w_i]) & \\text{if } w_i \\le w \\end{cases}$$\n\n**Part (b): DP Table $K[i, w]$ ($W = 0 \\dots 7$):**\n| Item | $w_i, v_i$ | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **0** | Base | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| **1** | (2, 3) | 0 | 0 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n| **2** | (3, 4) | 0 | 0 | 3 | 4 | 4 | 7 | 7 | 7 |\n| **3** | (4, 5) | 0 | 0 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 7 | 8 | 9 |\n| **4** | (5, 8) | 0 | 0 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 8 | 8 | **11** |\n\n**Maximum Value = 11**.\n\n**Part (c): Backtracking Items**\n- At $K[4, 7] = 11 \\ne K[3, 7] = 9 \\implies$ **Item 4 included** ($w_4=5, v_4=8$). Remaining $W = 7 - 5 = 2$.\n- At $K[3, 2] = 3 == K[2, 2] = 3 \\implies$ Item 3 not included.\n- At $K[2, 2] = 3 == K[1, 2] = 3 \\implies$ Item 2 not included.\n- At $K[1, 2] = 3 \\ne K[0, 2] = 0 \\implies$ **Item 1 included** ($w_1=2, v_1=3$). Remaining $W = 0$.\n**Selected Items:** $\\{\\text{Item 1}, \\text{Item 4}\\}$. Total Weight = $2 + 5 = 7 \\le 7$, Total Value = $3 + 8 = 11$.\n\n**Part (d): Why Greedy Fails on 0/1**\nGreedy sorts by $v_i/w_i$. If capacity $W=50$ and items are $A(w=10, v=60, r=6)$, $B(w=20, v=100, r=5)$, $C(w=30, v=120, r=4)$. Greedy picks $A+B$ (wt 30, val 160). But picking $B+C$ (wt 50, val 220) is vastly superior! Greedy leaves empty unusable capacity.', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2024 End-Sem Q5'}]}, '04_DSS': {'short': 'DSS', 'name': 'Discrete Structures', 'code': 'MAX-206', 'accent': '#e9d5ff', 'tagline': 'Logic, Relations, Posets, Recurrences & Graph Theory', 'units': [{'unit': 1, 'title': 'Propositional & Predicate Logic', 'weightage': '18%', 'topics': 'Truth tables, Tautologies, Valid arguments, Modus Ponens/Tollens, Quantifiers, CNF and DNF conversion', 'high_yield_tip': 'Proving an argument valid using rules of inference without truth tables is a frequent 5-mark question.'}, {'unit': 2, 'title': 'Set Theory, Relations & Posets', 'weightage': '22%', 'topics': 'Equivalence Relations, Equivalence Classes, Partial Orders, Hasse Diagrams, Lattices, Distributive & Complemented Lattices', 'high_yield_tip': 'Drawing Hasse diagram for divisibility relation $D_{30}$ or $D_{36}$ and finding LUB/GLB for all pairs.'}, {'unit': 3, 'title': 'Combinatorics & Pigeonhole Principle', 'weightage': '18%', 'topics': 'Generalized Pigeonhole Principle, Permutations/Combinations with repetition, Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion (PIE)', 'high_yield_tip': 'Generalized PHP: $\\lceil N / k \\rceil$ items guaranteed. Practice integer sum problems ($x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 15$).'}, {'unit': 4, 'title': 'Recurrence Relations & Generating Functions', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': 'Homogeneous linear recurrence with distinct & repeated roots, Particular solutions for polynomials & exponentials', 'high_yield_tip': 'Solving non-homogeneous recurrence: $a_n = a_n^{(h)} + a_n^{(p)}$ with initial conditions $a_0, a_1$.'}, {'unit': 5, 'title': 'Graph Theory & Trees', 'weightage': '22%', 'topics': "Handshaking Lemma, Euler Circuits vs Paths, Hamiltonian Cycles (Dirac & Ore), Planar Graphs (Euler's Formula $V-E+F=2$), Chromatic Number", 'high_yield_tip': 'Proving $K_5$ and $K_{3,3}$ non-planar using $3F \\le 2E \\implies E \\le 3V - 6$.'}], 'formulas': [{'title': 'Graph Handshaking Lemma', 'latex': '\\sum_{v \\in V} \\deg(v) = 2|E|', 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'explanation': 'The sum of degrees of all vertices in an undirected graph equals twice the number of edges. Consequently, the number of vertices with odd degree is ALWAYS even.', 'trap': "Don't forget self-loops add 2 to the degree of a vertex!", 'example': 'If a graph has 4 vertices of degree 3, 3 of degree 4, and 2 of degree 2: $\\sum \\deg(v) = 12 + 12 + 4 = 28 \\implies 2|E| = 28 \\implies |E| = 14$.'}, {'title': "Euler's Formula for Planar Graphs", 'latex': 'V - E + F = 2 \\quad \\implies \\quad E \\le 3V - 6 \\quad (V \\ge 3)', 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'explanation': 'For any connected planar graph with $V$ vertices, $E$ edges, and $F$ faces (including outer face). If triangle-free (like bipartite $K_{3,3}$): $E \\le 2V - 4$.', 'trap': 'Formula holds only for connected planar graphs. For $k$ connected components: $V - E + F = 1 + k$.', 'example': 'For $K_5$: $V=5, E = \\binom{5}{2} = 10$. But $3V - 6 = 15 - 6 = 9$. Since $10 > 9$, $K_5$ violates the inequality and is non-planar!'}, {'title': 'Generalized Pigeonhole Principle', 'latex': '\\text{If } N \\text{ items are placed into } k \\text{ boxes} \\implies \\text{At least one box has } \\ge \\left\\lceil \\frac{N}{k} \\right\\rceil \\text{ items}', 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'explanation': 'Guarantees a lower bound on the maximum number of items in any single container.', 'trap': 'Use ceiling function $\\lceil \\cdot \\rceil$, not floor function $\\lfloor \\cdot \\rfloor$.', 'example': 'In a group of 85 people, at least $\\lceil 85 / 12 \\rceil = \\lceil 7.08 \\rceil = 8$ people must share the same birth month.'}, {'title': 'Eulerian Graph Existence Conditions', 'latex': '\\begin{cases} \\text{Euler Circuit} & \\iff \\text{Connected and ALL vertices have EVEN degree} \\\\ \\text{Euler Path} & \\iff \\text{Connected and EXACTLY 2 vertices have ODD degree} \\end{cases}', 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'explanation': 'Eulerian circuit traverses every edge exactly once and returns to starting vertex. Euler path starts and ends at the two odd degree vertices.', 'trap': 'Do not confuse Eulerian graphs (traverses all edges) with Hamiltonian graphs (visits all vertices)!', 'example': 'Königsberg bridge graph had degrees 3, 3, 3, 5 (all 4 vertices odd) $\\implies$ Impossible to traverse.'}], 'flashcards': [{'q': 'What is the necessary and sufficient condition for a Poset to be a Lattice?', 'a': 'A Partially Ordered Set (Poset) $(L, \\le)$ is called a **Lattice** if and only if every pair of elements $\\{a, b\\} \\subseteq L$ has:\n1. A **Unique Least Upper Bound (LUB)**, denoted by $a \\vee b$ (Join / Supremum).\n2. A **Unique Greatest Lower Bound (GLB)**, denoted by $a \\wedge b$ (Meet / Infimum).', 'marks': '3–4 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}, {'q': "State Dirac's Theorem and Ore's Theorem for Hamiltonian graphs.", 'a': "• **Dirac's Theorem (1952)**: If $G$ is a simple graph with $n \\ge 3$ vertices such that $\\deg(v) \\ge n/2$ for every vertex $v$, then $G$ contains a Hamiltonian cycle.\n• **Ore's Theorem (1960)**: If $G$ is a simple graph with $n \\ge 3$ vertices such that for every pair of non-adjacent vertices $u, v$: $\\deg(u) + \\deg(v) \\ge n$, then $G$ contains a Hamiltonian cycle.", 'marks': '4 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}, {'q': 'State and prove the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion for 3 sets.', 'a': '$$|A \\cup B \\cup C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - (|A \\cap B| + |B \\cap C| + |A \\cap C|) + |A \\cap B \\cap C|$$\n**Proof intuition**: Summing individual sets counts elements in intersections twice, so subtract pairwise intersections. But elements in all 3 sets were added 3 times and subtracted 3 times (net 0), so add back $|A \\cap B \\cap C|$.', 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}], 'exam_questions': [{'id': 'dss_q1', 'marks': 2, 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'title': 'Equivalence Relation Axioms', 'question': 'Define an Equivalence Relation on a non-empty set $A$. State the three required properties with their mathematical formulations.', 'hint': 'Reflexive, Symmetric, Transitive.', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Definition as a binary relation satisfying three axiomatic properties', '1 Mark: Exact algebraic statements for Reflexivity, Symmetry, and Transitivity'], 'model_answer': 'A binary relation $R$ on set $A$ is an **Equivalence Relation** iff it satisfies:\n1. **Reflexivity**: $\\forall x \\in A, \\; (x, x) \\in R$.\n2. **Symmetry**: $\\forall x, y \\in A, \\; (x, y) \\in R \\implies (y, x) \\in R$.\n3. **Transitivity**: $\\forall x, y, z \\in A, \\; ((x, y) \\in R \\land (y, z) \\in R) \\implies (x, z) \\in R$.\nEquivalence relations partition set $A$ into mutually disjoint equivalence classes.', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Mid-Term Mathematics Core'}, {'id': 'dss_q2', 'marks': 5, 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'title': 'Solving Second-Order Linear Homogeneous Recurrence', 'question': 'Solve the recurrence relation:\n$$a_n = 5a_{n-1} - 6a_{n-2} \\quad \\text{for } n \\ge 2$$\nwith initial conditions $a_0 = 1$ and $a_1 = 4$.', 'hint': 'Form the characteristic equation r^2 - 5r + 6 = 0.', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Characteristic equation r^2 - 5r + 6 = 0', '1 Mark: Roots r_1 = 2, r_2 = 3', '1 Mark: General solution form a_n = C_1(2^n) + C_2(3^n)', '2 Marks: Solving for constants: C_1 = -1, C_2 = 2 => a_n = 2(3^n) - 2^n'], 'model_answer': '**Step 1: Characteristic Equation**\n$$r^2 - 5r + 6 = 0 \\implies (r - 2)(r - 3) = 0$$\nRoots: $r_1 = 2$, $r_2 = 3$ (distinct real roots).\n\n**Step 2: General Solution Form**\n$$a_n = C_1 \\cdot 2^n + C_2 \\cdot 3^n$$\n\n**Step 3: Solve for Constants using Initial Conditions**\n- For $n = 0$: $a_0 = C_1 \\cdot 2^0 + C_2 \\cdot 3^0 \\implies C_1 + C_2 = 1$.\n- For $n = 1$: $a_1 = C_1 \\cdot 2^1 + C_2 \\cdot 3^1 \\implies 2C_1 + 3C_2 = 4$.\n\nMultiply first equation by 2: $2C_1 + 2C_2 = 2$.\nSubtract: $(2C_1 + 3C_2) - (2C_1 + 2C_2) = 4 - 2 \\implies C_2 = 2$.\nThen $C_1 = 1 - 2 = -1$.\n\n**Final Closed-Form Solution:**\n$$a_n = 2 \\cdot 3^n - 2^n$$\n*Check:* $a_0 = 2(1) - 1 = 1$; $a_1 = 2(3) - 2 = 4$; $a_2 = 5(4) - 6(1) = 14$ vs $2(9) - 4 = 14$. Correct!', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2025 End-Sem Q2'}, {'id': 'dss_q3', 'marks': 10, 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'title': 'Graph Theory: Eulerian vs Hamiltonian Cycles & Planarity', 'question': "(a) State Euler's Theorem on Eulerian graphs. Give the necessary and sufficient conditions for a connected graph to contain an Eulerian circuit.\n(b) Prove that the complete bipartite graph $K_{3,3}$ is non-planar using Euler's formula $V - E + F = 2$.\n(c) Determine whether the Petersen graph is Hamiltonian, Eulerian, or neither. Justify.", 'hint': 'In bipartite graphs, every face has at least 4 boundary edges (2E >= 4F).', 'marking_scheme': ["3 Marks: Euler's theorem: connected graph has Euler circuit iff every vertex has even degree", '4 Marks: Planarity proof for K_{3,3}: E=9, V=6. If planar, F = 2 - 6 + 9 = 5. But 2E >= 4F => 18 >= 20 (contradiction!)', '3 Marks: Petersen graph analysis: 3-regular (odd degrees => not Eulerian); lacks Hamiltonian cycle'], 'model_answer': "**Part (a): Euler's Theorem**\n- A connected graph $G$ has an **Eulerian Circuit** (traverses every edge exactly once and returns to start) if and only if **every vertex in $G$ has an EVEN degree**.\n- A connected graph has an **Eulerian Path** (open) if and only if it has **exactly two vertices of odd degree**.\n\n**Part (b): Non-Planarity Proof of $K_{3,3}$**\n- For $K_{3,3}$: Vertices $V = 3 + 3 = 6$, Edges $E = 3 \\times 3 = 9$.\n- Assume $K_{3,3}$ is planar. By Euler's Planar Formula:\n  $$V - E + F = 2 \\implies 6 - 9 + F = 2 \\implies F = 5$$\n- Since $K_{3,3}$ is bipartite, it contains no odd cycles (no triangles). Every face is bounded by at least 4 edges:\n  $$2E \\ge 4F \\implies 2(9) \\ge 4(5) \\implies 18 \\ge 20 \\quad \\text{\\textbf{(CONTRADICTION!)}}$$\nHence, **$K_{3,3}$ is strictly non-planar** (Kuratowski's second sub-graph).\n\n**Part (c): Petersen Graph**\n- **Eulerian:** The Petersen graph is 3-regular (every vertex has degree 3). Since degrees are odd, it is **NOT Eulerian**.\n- **Hamiltonian:** The Petersen graph is one of the most famous examples of a hypohamiltonian graph: it has **NO Hamiltonian cycle** (removing any vertex yields a Hamiltonian graph, but the graph itself has none).", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2024 End-Sem Q4'}]}, '05_OOPS': {'short': 'OOPS', 'name': 'Object-Oriented Programming (C++)', 'code': 'CSX-203 / ITX-203', 'accent': '#fecdd3', 'tagline': 'C++ Classes, Virtual Tables, Templates, STL & Memory', 'units': [{'unit': 1, 'title': 'Classes, Constructors & Destructors', 'weightage': '18%', 'topics': 'Constructors (Default, Parameterized, Copy, Move), Destructors, `this` pointer, Static members, Constant member functions', 'high_yield_tip': 'Copy constructor signature: `ClassName(const ClassName& other)` - passing by value causes infinite recursion!'}, {'unit': 2, 'title': 'Dynamic Memory & Deep vs Shallow Copy', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': 'Dynamic Memory Allocation (`new`/`delete` vs `malloc`/`free`), Copy Assignment Operator, Rule of 3 / Rule of 5, Friend Functions', 'high_yield_tip': 'Dangling pointer and double-free crash caused by shallow copy of pointers. Always implement custom deep copy.'}, {'unit': 3, 'title': 'Inheritance & The Diamond Problem', 'weightage': '22%', 'topics': 'Access Specifiers in Inheritance, Multiple & Hybrid Inheritance, Diamond Problem, Virtual Base Classes, Object Slicing', 'high_yield_tip': '`virtual` keyword in inheritance (`class B : virtual public A`) eliminates duplicate subobjects in the diamond problem.'}, {'unit': 4, 'title': 'Polymorphism & vtable/vptr Mechanics', 'weightage': '24%', 'topics': 'Compile-time Overloading vs Runtime Overriding, Virtual Functions, Pure Virtual Functions, Abstract Classes, vtable & vptr layout', 'high_yield_tip': 'Draw the exact memory layout of `vptr` pointing to `vtable` function pointer array.'}, {'unit': 5, 'title': 'Templates, STL & Smart Pointers', 'weightage': '16%', 'topics': 'Function & Class Templates, STL Vector/Map, Exception Handling (`try-catch`), RAII, Smart Pointers (`unique_ptr`, `shared_ptr`)', 'high_yield_tip': 'Why `shared_ptr` circular references cause memory leaks and how `weak_ptr` breaks the cycle.'}], 'formulas': [{'title': 'vtable and vptr Memory Dispatch Layout', 'latex': '\\text{Object Memory} = [\\; \\text{vptr} \\;] \\longrightarrow \\text{vtable: } [\\; \\&\\text{Derived::func1}, \\; \\&\\text{Derived::func2} \\;]', 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'explanation': 'Every class with at least one `virtual` function has a compiler-generated `vtable` (array of function pointers). Each object instance contains a hidden `vptr` pointer (8 bytes on 64-bit systems) pointing to that vtable.', 'trap': 'Virtual dispatch only occurs when invoking methods through a pointer (`Base*`) or reference (`Base&`), NEVER with value semantics!', 'example': 'Calling `ptr->show()` translates internally to: `(*(ptr->vptr[0]))(ptr);`.'}, {'title': 'Rule of 5 (Modern C++ Resource Management)', 'latex': '\\{\\text{Destructor}, \\; \\text{Copy Constr}, \\; \\text{Copy Assign}, \\; \\text{Move Constr}, \\; \\text{Move Assign}\\}', 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'explanation': 'If a class manages raw resources (pointers, file handles), declaring any one of these requires explicitly managing all five to prevent leaks and double-free bugs.', 'trap': 'Move constructor must be marked `noexcept` so `std::vector` reallocations use move instead of copying!', 'example': '`Widget(Widget&& other) noexcept : data(other.data) { other.data = nullptr; }`'}, {'title': 'Diamond Problem Virtual Base Class Syntax', 'latex': '\\text{class Derived1 : virtual public Base}; \\quad \\text{class Derived2 : virtual public Base};', 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'explanation': 'Ensures that the most-derived class (`Bottom`) contains only a single shared instance of `Base`, resolving ambiguity.', 'trap': 'The most derived class constructor is directly responsible for invoking the `virtual public Base` constructor!', 'example': '`class Diamond : public Derived1, public Derived2 { Diamond() : Base(), Derived1(), Derived2() {} };`'}], 'flashcards': [{'q': 'Why must base class destructors always be declared `virtual`?', 'a': 'When deleting a derived class object through a base class pointer (`Base* p = new Derived(); delete p;`):\n• If the destructor is **non-virtual**, C++ performs static binding and calls ONLY `~Base()`. The `~Derived()` destructor is never executed, causing memory leaks for any resources allocated by `Derived`.\n• If declared `virtual ~Base()`, runtime dispatch resolves to `~Derived()`, which properly cleans up derived resources and then calls `~Base()`.', 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Guaranteed 5-mark question'}, {'q': 'What is Object Slicing in C++ and how can it be avoided?', 'a': 'Object slicing occurs when a derived class object is assigned or passed by value to a base class variable:\n`Derived d; Base b = d;`\nThe base copy constructor copies only the base class portion of `d`, slicing off and discarding all derived member variables and derived virtual function overrides.\n**Solution**: Always pass polymorphic objects by pointer (`Base*`) or reference (`const Base&`).', 'marks': '4 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}, {'q': 'Differentiate between `std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`, and `std::weak_ptr`.', 'a': '• `std::unique_ptr`: Sole ownership of heap object. Zero overhead over raw pointer. Cannot be copied, only moved (`std::move`).\n• `std::shared_ptr`: Reference-counted shared ownership. Increments ref count on copy, decrements on destruction. Deallocates when ref count hits 0.\n• `std::weak_ptr`: Non-owning observer of a `shared_ptr`. Does not increase reference count. Used to break cyclic reference leaks.', 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Very High'}], 'exam_questions': [{'id': 'oops_q1', 'marks': 2, 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'title': 'Need for Virtual Destructors in C++', 'question': 'Why should a base class destructor always be declared `virtual` when working with polymorphic hierarchies? What happens if it is non-virtual?', 'hint': 'Consider deleting a derived class object through a base class pointer: `Base* ptr = new Derived(); delete ptr;`.', 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: Explaining that base destructor must be virtual to invoke derived destructor first', '1 Mark: Stating that non-virtual destructor results in Undefined Behavior and Resource/Memory Leaks'], 'model_answer': 'When a derived class object is deleted through a base class pointer (`Base* p = new Derived(); delete p;`):\n• If the Base destructor is **non-virtual**, the compiler uses static binding and calls **only the Base destructor**. The Derived class destructor is NEVER executed!\n• **Consequence:** Memory leaks, dangling file handles, and Undefined Behavior for resources allocated in `Derived`.\n• Marking it `virtual (~Base() {})` ensures dynamic dispatch via `vtable`, correctly executing `~Derived()` first, then `~Base()`.', 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar C++ Object Architecture Core'}, {'id': 'oops_q2', 'marks': 5, 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'title': 'Virtual Table (`vtable`) & Pointer (`vptr`) Memory Mechanics', 'question': 'Explain how C++ achieves runtime polymorphism under the hood. Draw the memory layout of an object with virtual functions, showing the role of `vptr` and the class `vtable`.', 'hint': 'vptr is inserted as hidden first member of the object; vtable is per-class array of function pointers.', 'marking_scheme': ['2 Marks: Explanation of vtable (static per-class table of function pointers) and vptr (per-object pointer)', '2 Marks: Memory layout diagram showing object pointing to vtable in read-only memory', '1 Mark: Dispatch cost explanation: dynamic call resolved as (*(obj->vptr)[offset])()'], 'model_answer': "**Mechanics of C++ Dynamic Dispatch:**\n1. **`vtable` (Virtual Table):** A static array of function pointers created by the compiler once per polymorphic class during compilation, stored in read-only data segment (`.rodata`).\n2. **`vptr` (Virtual Pointer):** A hidden pointer injected as the very first member of any object of a class containing at least one virtual function. It is initialized in the constructor to point to that class's `vtable`.\n\n```\nObject in Heap/Stack:          vtable in Read-Only Memory:\n+--------------------+         +-----------------------------+\n| vptr ------------->|-------->| [0]: &Base::func1()         |\n+--------------------+         | [1]: &Derived::func2() (ovr)|\n| member variables   |         +-----------------------------+\n+--------------------+\n```\n\n**Dispatch Cost:** A virtual function call `p->func2()` resolves to:\n`(*(p->vptr[1]))(p)`\nCost: 1 pointer dereference + 1 array index lookup. Near-zero runtime penalty!", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2025 End-Sem Q4'}, {'id': 'oops_q3', 'marks': 10, 'unit': 'Unit 4', 'title': 'Multiple Inheritance Diamond Problem & Virtual Base Classes', 'question': "(a) What is the 'Diamond Problem' in C++ Multiple Inheritance? Provide a clean code example demonstrating the resulting compilation ambiguity.\n(b) How does `virtual` base class inheritance resolve this ambiguity? Explain the memory overhead.\n(c) What is the initialization responsibility rule for a `virtual` base class constructor?", 'hint': 'A -> B, A -> C; D inherits from both B and C. D contains two copies of A.', 'marking_scheme': ['3 Marks: Code demonstration showing ambiguous call d.data or d.show()', '4 Marks: Solution using virtual inheritance: class B : virtual public A; class C : virtual public A', "3 Marks: Constructor rule: the most derived class (D) is directly responsible for invoking A's constructor"], 'model_answer': "**Part (a): The Diamond Problem**\nOccurs when class `D` inherits from classes `B` and `C`, both of which inherit from a common base class `A`:\n```cpp\nclass A { public: int val; };\nclass B : public A {};\nclass C : public A {};\nclass D : public B, public C {};\n\nD obj;\nobj.val = 10; // COMPILE ERROR: Request for member 'val' is ambiguous!\n```\n`obj` has TWO sub-objects of `A` (`B::A` and `C::A`).\n\n**Part (b): Resolution via `virtual` Base Class**\nBy declaring `virtual public A`, the compiler collapses multiple `A` sub-objects into a **single shared instance**:\n```cpp\nclass B : virtual public A {};\nclass C : virtual public A {};\nclass D : public B, public C {};\n```\n`obj.val = 10;` is now completely unambiguous!\n- **Memory Overhead:** Extra indirection pointers (`vbptr` or virtual base offsets in the `vtable`) to locate the shared `A` sub-object.\n\n**Part (c): Constructor Responsibility Rule**\nIn virtual inheritance, intermediate classes `B` and `C` **DO NOT** call `A`'s constructor. Instead, **the most derived class (`D`) is directly responsible** for invoking `A`'s constructor in its initialization list:\n```cpp\nD(int x) : A(x), B(), C() {} // D directly initializes shared A\n```", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2024 End-Sem Q3'}]}, '06_WT': {'short': 'WT', 'name': 'Web Technologies', 'code': 'ITX-207', 'accent': '#fef08a', 'tagline': 'HTML5, CSS3 Grid/Flex, JS Event Loop, Node & React', 'units': [{'unit': 1, 'title': 'Web Architecture, HTTP & HTML5', 'weightage': '15%', 'topics': 'HTTP vs HTTPS, Request/Response headers, Status codes (200, 301, 400, 401, 403, 404, 500), Semantic HTML5 elements, Forms', 'high_yield_tip': 'Differentiate HTTP methods: GET (idempotent, query params in URL) vs POST (non-idempotent, payload in body).'}, {'unit': 2, 'title': 'CSS3 Styling, Flexbox & Responsive Grid', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': 'CSS Box Model (`box-sizing`), Specificity calculation, Flexbox axes (`justify-content`, `align-items`), CSS Grid (`fr`), Media Queries', 'high_yield_tip': '`box-sizing: border-box` includes padding & border inside total width/height. Specificity: `!important` > inline > ID > class > tag.'}, {'unit': 3, 'title': 'JavaScript Engine, Closures & Event Loop', 'weightage': '25%', 'topics': 'Call Stack, Web APIs, Microtask (Promises) vs Macrotask Queue (`setTimeout`), Execution Context, Hoisting, Closures, Prototypes', 'high_yield_tip': 'Code output question predicting the exact print order of `console.log`, `setTimeout`, and `Promise.resolve().then()`.'}, {'unit': 4, 'title': 'DOM Manipulation & Event Propagation', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': 'DOM Tree traversal, Event Bubbling vs Capturing, Event Delegation, AJAX, Fetch API, localStorage vs sessionStorage vs Cookies', 'high_yield_tip': 'Explain Event Delegation: attaching a single listener to a common ancestor using `e.target`.'}, {'unit': 5, 'title': 'Backend Architecture, Node.js & React', 'weightage': '20%', 'topics': 'Node.js Single-Threaded Event Loop (libuv), Express Middleware pipeline, RESTful API design, React Virtual DOM & Hooks', 'high_yield_tip': 'React Virtual DOM reconciliation (Diffing algorithm) and why React is faster than direct DOM manipulation.'}], 'formulas': [{'title': 'JavaScript Event Loop Priority Hierarchy', 'latex': '\\text{Call Stack} \\longrightarrow \\text{Microtask Queue (Promises, queueMicrotask)} \\longrightarrow \\text{Macrotask Queue (setTimeout, setInterval, I/O)}', 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'explanation': 'Synchronous code runs to completion on the Call Stack. Before taking the next Macrotask, the Event Loop empties ALL Microtasks currently queued!', 'trap': '`setTimeout(fn, 0)` is placed in the Macrotask queue and will ALWAYS execute AFTER all resolved Promise `.then()` callbacks!', 'example': '`console.log(1); setTimeout(() => console.log(2), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3)); console.log(4);` Prints: 1, 4, 3, 2.'}, {'title': 'CSS Box Model Total Width Calculation', 'latex': '\\text{Total Width} = \\text{width} + \\text{padding-left/right} + \\text{border-left/right} + \\text{margin-left/right}', 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'explanation': 'Under standard `content-box`, padding and border add to the specified width. Under `border-box`, specified `width` encompasses content + padding + border.', 'trap': 'Collapsing vertical margins occur only between adjacent block elements, never on horizontal margins or flex/grid items!', 'example': 'A 200px div with 20px padding and 5px border: `content-box` total = 200 + 40 + 10 = 250px; `border-box` total = 200px.'}, {'title': 'CSS Specificity Calculation Formula', 'latex': '\\text{Score} = (\\text{Inline}, \\; \\text{ID}, \\; \\text{Class/Pseudo-class/Attr}, \\; \\text{Element/Pseudo-element})', 'unit': 'Unit 2', 'explanation': 'Inline styles = (1, 0, 0, 0); ID selectors = (0, 1, 0, 0); Classes = (0, 0, 1, 0); Tags = (0, 0, 0, 1). Compare left to right.', 'trap': '100 classes can NEVER override a single ID selector in standard CSS specificity!', 'example': '`nav#main ul.menu li.active a` has specificity `(0, 1, 2, 2)`.'}], 'flashcards': [{'q': 'What is a Closure in JavaScript? Give an exam code snippet demonstrating data privacy.', 'a': "A **Closure** is the combination of a function bundled together with references to its surrounding lexical state (lexical environment). It gives an inner function access to an outer function's scope even after the outer function has closed.\n```javascript\nfunction createCounter() {\n  let count = 0; // private variable\n  return {\n    increment: () => ++count,\n    get: () => count\n  };\n}\nconst c = createCounter();\nc.increment(); // 1\nconsole.log(c.count); // undefined (encapsulated!)\n```", 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Extremely High (Standard JS question)'}, {'q': 'Explain Event Bubbling vs Event Capturing. What is Event Delegation?', 'a': "• **Capturing Phase**: Event trickles down from `window` through the DOM hierarchy to the target element.\n• **Target Phase**: Event triggers on the actual clicked element.\n• **Bubbling Phase**: Event bubbles upward from target back to `window` (default in `addEventListener`).\n• **Event Delegation**: Technique of adding a single event listener to a parent container instead of attaching listeners to hundreds of child elements, taking advantage of event bubbling: `parent.addEventListener('click', (e) => { if (e.target.matches('.item')) ... });`.", 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'Very High'}, {'q': "How does React's Virtual DOM reconciliation diffing algorithm achieve $O(n)$ performance?", 'a': 'Comparing two arbitrary trees has a general complexity of $O(n^3)$. React reduces this to $O(n)$ using two heuristic assumptions:\n1. Two elements of different types will produce completely different trees (replaces old subtree entirely).\n2. The developer can hint which child elements may be stable across different renders with a unique `key` prop, allowing React to match and reorder nodes rather than re-creating them.', 'marks': '5 Marks', 'frequency': 'High'}], 'exam_questions': [{'id': 'wt_q1', 'marks': 2, 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'title': 'Equality Operators: `==` vs `===` in JavaScript', 'question': 'Differentiate between abstract equality (`==`) and strict equality (`===`) in JavaScript. What is type coercion?', 'hint': "Consider '5' == 5 vs '5' === 5 and null == undefined vs null === undefined.", 'marking_scheme': ['1 Mark: == performs implicit type coercion; === checks both value and type without coercion', '1 Mark: Concrete examples demonstrating behavior'], 'model_answer': "• **`==` (Abstract Equality)**: Compares two values for equality **after converting (coercing)** both values to a common type according to the ECMAScript ToPrimitive/ToNumber rules.\n  - Example: `'5' == 5` is `true`; `null == undefined` is `true`; `0 == false` is `true`.\n• **`===` (Strict Equality)**: Compares values **without type coercion**. Returns `true` if and only if both the type and the value are identical.\n  - Example: `'5' === 5` is `false`; `null === undefined` is `false`; `0 === false` is `false`.\nAlways prefer `===` in production to prevent unexpected coercion bugs.", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Web Tech Core'}, {'id': 'wt_q2', 'marks': 5, 'unit': 'Unit 3', 'title': 'JavaScript Event Loop Execution Order Trace', 'question': "Predict the exact output of the following JavaScript snippet and explain the event loop priority rules governing Call Stack, Microtasks (Promises), and Macrotasks (`setTimeout`):\n```javascript\nconsole.log('1');\nsetTimeout(() => console.log('2'), 0);\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3')).then(() => console.log('4'));\nconsole.log('5');\n```", 'hint': 'Synchronous code runs first. Then microtask queue is completely drained. Then next macrotask runs.', 'marking_scheme': ['2 Marks: Correct execution output: 1, 5, 3, 4, 2', '3 Marks: Thorough explanation of Call Stack, Microtask queue priority, and Macrotask queue processing'], 'model_answer': "**Exact Output:**\n`1`\n`5`\n`3`\n`4`\n`2`\n\n**Step-by-Step Event Loop Walkthrough:**\n1. `console.log('1')` executes synchronously on the **Call Stack** $\\implies$ Prints `1`.\n2. `setTimeout(..., 0)` registers a timer with Web APIs. Its callback is placed into the **Macrotask Queue**.\n3. `Promise.resolve().then(...)` schedules its `.then()` callback into the **Microtask Queue**.\n4. `console.log('5')` executes synchronously on the **Call Stack** $\\implies$ Prints `5`.\n5. Call Stack is now empty! Before checking Macrotasks, the Event Loop **empties ALL Microtasks**:\n   - Microtask 1: Prints `3`, returns undefined, chaining the second `.then()` onto the Microtask Queue.\n   - Microtask 2: Prints `4`.\n6. Microtask queue is now empty. Event Loop picks the first Macrotask:\n   - `setTimeout` callback executes $\\implies$ Prints `2`.", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2025 End-Sem Q3'}, {'id': 'wt_q3', 'marks': 10, 'unit': 'Unit 5', 'title': 'React Architecture: Virtual DOM, Reconciliation & Diffing', 'question': "(a) What is the Virtual DOM in React? Why is direct manipulation of the real browser DOM slow?\n(b) Explain React's $O(n)$ Reconciliation (Diffing) algorithm and its two heuristic rules.\n(c) Why is using array index as a `key` prop considered an anti-pattern? Give an example of visual bug it causes.", 'hint': 'Heuristics: different element types produce different trees; keys match children across renders.', 'marking_scheme': ['3 Marks: Virtual DOM definition as lightweight JS object tree; explanation of browser layout/reflow bottlenecks', '4 Marks: Diffing heuristics: element type comparison and key-based reconciliation reducing O(n^3) to O(n)', '3 Marks: Index-as-key anti-pattern: state corruption when inserting/reordering items in dynamic lists'], 'model_answer': "**Part (a): Virtual DOM & DOM Performance Bottleneck**\n- The **Virtual DOM (VDOM)** is an in-memory lightweight JavaScript object representation of the real DOM (e.g. `{ type: 'div', props: { className: 'card' }, children: [...] }`).\n- **Why Real DOM is Slow:** Manipulating real DOM nodes triggers browser **Reflow (Layout recalculation)** and **Repaint (Rasterization)** across the render tree. Batching updates in JavaScript VDOM computes the minimal set of real DOM operations before touching the browser layout engine.\n\n**Part (b): React's $O(n)$ Diffing Heuristics**\nStandard tree diffing takes $O(n^3)$ operations. React achieves $O(n)$ via two heuristics:\n1. **Different Element Types:** If two root elements have different types (e.g., `<div>` changes to `<span>`), React tears down the old tree and mounts the new one from scratch.\n2. **Keys on Child Elements:** When children change, React uses the `key` attribute to match children in the original tree with children in the new tree, allowing efficient reordering instead of full recreation.\n\n**Part (c): Index as Key Anti-Pattern**\nWhen using index (`key={index}`), if an item is prepended or removed from the list, the index of all remaining items changes:\n- React matches by key: item at index 0 is compared to new index 0.\n- **Consequence:** Uncontrolled component state (e.g., text inputs, checkboxes) remains attached to the index rather than the data item, causing input values to display on the wrong rows after deletion/reordering!", 'exam_source': 'NIT Jalandhar Dec 2024 End-Sem Q5'}]}}
